INT. GUARD BOOTH - GRANGE COLD STORAGE - NIGHT

Rain crawling down plexiglass. MARA VOSS, thirties, coat a size too big, holds her badge to the reader.

Red light.

She tries again. Red light.

A hand comes past her shoulder with a different badge. Green.

DELACROIX, fifties, dry in all that rain.

DELACROIX
They re-cut the strips every quarter. Nobody remembers to tell the night people.

MARA
You're not night people.

DELACROIX
I'm everybody's people.

He holds the door. She goes through under his arm.

MARA
Sign yourself out when you go. Owen gets audited on the door log, not you.

DELACROIX
Owen gets audited on everything.

INT. GUARD OFFICE - NIGHT

Four monitors, one dead. Mara hangs the wet coat. A radio crackles.

OWEN (V.O.)
Booth to office. You're four minutes late on the hour.

MARA
The door ate me.

OWEN (V.O.)
The door ate you.

MARA
Ask Delacroix.

A pause on the line. Longer than it needs to be.

OWEN (V.O.)
Delacroix is off rotation since Thursday.

Mara looks at the door log on the screen. Scrolls. Her own name at 11:04. Nothing above it.

MARA
Yeah. That's what I thought.

INT. COLD ROOM - AISLE FOUR - NIGHT

Breath in the air. Blue pallets stacked to the ceiling, shrink-wrapped, numbered.

Mara walks the aisle with a clipboard, touching each stack as she counts. Her lips move.

She stops. Looks up. Counts again from the far end, walking backward.

Forty-one.

She turns the clipboard around. The manifest says thirty-eight.

She stands in the cold a while, not writing anything down.

INT. GUARD OFFICE - NIGHT

Delacroix is in her chair with her coffee. He does not get up.

DELACROIX
Aisle four runs long. Always has. Receiving stacks the overflow there when the dock is wet, then nobody re-keys it.

MARA
Three pallets of overflow.

DELACROIX
Three, five. It's a warehouse, not a bank.

MARA
It's a bank if you count it.

He smiles at that, as though she has said something charming.

DELACROIX
You're going to be very good at this.

MARA
At counting.

DELACROIX
At knowing when to stop.

He sets the coffee down, still full, and leaves it steaming on her desk.

INT. COLD ROOM - AISLE FOUR - NIGHT

Mara back with a box cutter. She slits the wrap on the last stack, one clean line.

Underneath the blue film: a second label. Older. A different consignee, a different lot code, still legible under the new one.

She peels it slowly, whole, and holds it up to the light.

Beat.

She could photograph it. She could radio it.

She folds it once and puts it in her sock.

Then she re-wraps the pallet, pulls the loose film tight, and walks out with the cut edge turned to the wall.

INT. NORTH CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Mara comes around the corner into Delacroix, who is standing perfectly still in the dark, looking at nothing.

DELACROIX
Door log's got a hole in it tonight.

MARA
Does it.

DELACROIX
Eleven o'clock. Somebody came in on a card and the card didn't write.

MARA
Then get the card fixed.

DELACROIX
That's what I'm doing.

He puts a hand on her shoulder, friendly, and she lets him, and when he takes it away his badge is no longer clipped to his belt, and neither of them says anything about it.

EXT. LOADING DOCK - NIGHT

Headlights. A tractor unit backs a trailer into bay three with no beeper, no reverse lamps, a man walking it in on a phone torch.

Mara on the mezzanine, in the dark, watching.

She checks the schedule taped to the rail. Bay three: empty until 06:00.

The dock door rolls up. Blue pallets go out. She counts them.

Three.

INT. COLD ROOM - AISLE FOUR - NIGHT

Mara moving fast down the aisle with the clipboard.

Behind her the door shuts. The bolt throws.

She reaches it, hits the release. Nothing. Hits it again.

Through the wire glass: Delacroix, close enough to touch, breathing on the other side of it.

DELACROIX
It's four hours to shift change. You'll be cold, that's all. Cold and quiet, and then you'll be somebody who was cold once.

MARA
Sign yourself out.

DELACROIX
What?

MARA
When you go. Sign yourself out.

INT. COLD ROOM - INTERIOR DOOR - NIGHT

Mara at the far end of the aisle, past the racking, at the small interior door to the compressor gallery. A reader beside it, glowing red.

She takes Delacroix's badge out of her sleeve.

Green.

EXT. LOT - DAWN

Grey light, rain finished. OWEN, twenties, out of his car with two coffees and nowhere to put them.

Mara comes across the lot shaking, lips blue, and hands him a folded label.

OWEN
What is this.

MARA
Aisle four. Under the wrap. It carries a lot code that was written off in March.

Behind them, an engine. Delacroix's car pulling out of the far row, unhurried, one hand raised in the window as he passes.

Owen starts after it.

MARA (CONT'D)
Let him.

OWEN
He's leaving.

MARA
He's leaving on his own card. It writes now.

She takes one of the coffees. Holds it against her face for the heat and does not drink it.
